  "text": "Rain is a fundamental component of Earth\u2019s weather systems, formed through a continuous process known as the water cycle. This cycle begins with evaporation, where the sun\u2019s heat causes water from oceans, lakes, and rivers to turn into invisible water vapor. As this vapor rises into the cooler upper layers of the atmosphere, it condenses around tiny particles of dust or pollen, forming microscopic water droplets. These droplets cluster together to create clouds. When the droplets grow heavy enough, gravity pulls them back to the Earth as precipitation, which we experience as rain.\n\nThis natural process is vital for sustaining life on land, particularly for plants and the health of river systems. For plants, rain is the primary source of fresh water. When rain falls, it penetrates the soil, providing the moisture necessary for roots to absorb nutrients. This water is then transported through the plant\u2019s vascular system to its leaves, where it plays a crucial role in photosynthesis\u2014the process by which plants convert sunlight into energy. Without regular rainfall, plants would wither, leading to a collapse in the food chain that supports animals and humans alike.\n\nRivers also depend heavily on the water cycle. Rainwater that falls on land does not always stay in one place. Some of it soaks into",
